+# S1P — implementation log
+
+What was built, in the order it was built, and what went wrong on the way. **The failures are
+recorded because a log that only records successes is a log nobody can learn from.**
+
+---
+
+## 1. Baseline
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| required | `d897833` |
+| actual | `d897833b14253030f75fe113534df34a9827d464` |
+| branch | `feature/s1o-provider-selection-audit-boundary` (continued; no new branch) |
+| pre-existing worktree changes | **YES — two untracked review artifacts**, `S1O-review.diff` (9.1 MB) and `S1O-correction-review.diff` (1.1 MB) |
+| what was done about them | **NOTHING.** They are the owner's review material. Not reset, not discarded, not stashed, not absorbed |
+
+The recorded S1O verification — 184 files, 2654 tests, 0 failed, typecheck green, lint green —
+was taken as the baseline and re-measured at the end rather than re-measured first: the
+baseline run was started in the background before any edit and did not complete within the
+tool's window, so the FINAL run is the one reported and the S1O record is the one it is
+compared against.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The gap analysis, and the one thing it found that changed the slice
+
+`§7` required a gap analysis before coding. The mechanical part was unsurprising: S1N and S1O
+had already built every boundary S1P needs, and the audit read protocol was **already
+SendGrid-shaped** — `MESSAGE_ACTIVITY_SEARCH`, `MESSAGE_ACTIVITY_DETAIL`,
+`MESSAGE_ACTIVITY_COUNT`, a mandatory period bound and an optional correlation tag. S1O built
+it generically and it needed no widening.
+
+**THEN THE CATALOGUE.**
+
+```
+$ grep -o '"adapter"[^,]*' artifacts/control/class-03.action-catalogue.json | sort -u
+"adapter": "mock_ads"
+"adapter": "mock_commerce"
+"adapter": "mock_processor"
+```
+
+Four action classes, three adapters, **no email anything**. `createAdapterRuntimeRegistry`
+requires the adapter identity to be one the VERIFIED class-3 catalogue names, and
+`artifactParsers.ts` requires the catalogue to be exhaustive over `ACTION_CLASSES` in both
+directions — so a fifth TypeScript member forces a fifth signed catalogue record, which
+reaches class 2 (Cedar), class 19 (constructors, whose key migration is an open obligation),
+the manifest, the deployment pin and the accepted architecture verification.
+
+**That is not a bounded slice, and the recoverability of "send an email" is an owner ruling
+rather than an implementation detail.** Recorded as `S1P-C1`, and it is why the live portion
+would remain PARTIAL even if an account existed today.
+
+**AND THE CREDENTIAL CHECK CAME BACK EMPTY.** No `.env`, no `SENDGRID_*` or `SG_*` variable,
+no key anywhere in the tree. State A, decided before the first line was written.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The placement decision, which took longer than any code
+
+Three candidate locations, and the reasoning is in `S1P-C2`:
+
+| | verdict |
+|---|---|
+| `src/` | **NO.** Three accepted suites forbid it, S1M's own sentence explains why widening them casually is a defect, and a live vendor client in the production control-plane tree is one `fork` from a production deployment |
+| `tests/` | **NO.** `§18` reserves `TEST_ONLY` for a "test synthetic provider". Putting the first real external-write site in the scope excluded from the production perimeter count is the exact weakening `48 §7` question 4 exists to catch |
+| `validation/` | **YES.** Fourth perimeter root, first path segment is not `tests`, so PRODUCTION scope and an unannotated site fails the build |
+
+The consequence was three accepted assertions to amend, and `45 §3` says how: out loud, in the
+file, with the reasoning beside them. Done, and the amended forms are strictly stronger than
+the sentences they replace.
+
+---
+
+## 4. What went wrong, in order
+
+**1. `tsc --noEmit` passed without checking anything.**
+The first typecheck after writing four files returned exit 0 in seconds. `tsconfig.json`'s
+`include` lists `src`, `tests`, `tools`, `spikes` and `*.config.ts` — **not `validation`** — so
+the compiler was not reading the new tree at all. Caught by
+`tsc --noEmit --listFiles | grep -c validation` returning 0. `validation/**/*.ts` was added to
+`include` with the reasoning beside `tools/**`'s.
+
+A typecheck that passes because it read nothing is worse than one that fails.
+
+**2. The kill-point adapter would not compile.**
+`export { integrationAdapter_killPoint3 as integrationAdapter }` collided with the imported
+`integrationAdapter` from `./adapter.js`. Fixed by importing as `realSendGridAdapter` and
+naming the export directly — which is also clearer: the wrapper's export has the SAME name as
+the real adapter's, and that is what lets a descriptor swap one for the other by changing a
+module specifier and nothing else.
+
+**3. `cli.ts` self-invoked on import.**
+The first version carried a `import.meta.url` / `process.argv[1]` guard so the module could be
+both a library and an entry point. It is exactly the kind of clever line that silently stops
+guarding after a path change, and the offline suite needs to import `establishFacts`. Replaced
+with `run.ts`, a five-line entry point whose only job is to call `main`, and an assertion that
+`cli.ts` contains no top-level `void main(`.
+
+**4. The harness re-bootstrapped over a live kernel.**
+`establishFacts` called `bootstrapControlArtifactAuthority()` unconditionally, which in the
+test worker raised seven `CRITICAL` `TRUST_CONFIG_MISSING` incidents per run — the worker HAD
+a bootstrapped kernel, from a test fixture's configuration source, and the harness was reading
+`process.env` and finding nothing. Changed to prefer `activeVerifiedControlArtifacts()` when
+`kernelAuthorityReady()`, which is both quieter and more correct: re-reading the trust
+configuration and publishing the second answer is not the harness's business.
+
+**5. A boundary scan that found itself.**
