+# S1P — operator procedure for the live Twilio SendGrid non-production validation
+
+**NOTHING IN THIS DOCUMENT HAS BEEN PERFORMED.** No SendGrid account was created, no
+credential was provisioned, and no provider request of any kind was made. This is the
+procedure a future live run follows, written so that each step names the mechanism that
+checks it rather than asking the operator to be careful.
+
+> **NEVER PASTE A KEY INTO THIS FILE, INTO ANY SOURCE FILE, INTO A COMMAND LINE, INTO A
+> COMMIT MESSAGE, INTO AN ISSUE TRACKER OR INTO CHAT OUTPUT.** Every step below moves a key
+> only from the provider's own console into a deployment document on the host that will run
+> the harness. The repository never sees one, and `.gitignore` carries the patterns for the
+> case where a locator is pointed at a path inside the working tree anyway.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Before anything: what S1P does NOT authorise
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| production SendGrid | **NOT ENABLED, and not enabled by this procedure.** |
+| a customer recipient | **IMPOSSIBLE by construction.** The recipient is launch configuration; `DispatchRequest`'s closed twenty-field list carries no recipient member, and `buildSendGridSendRequest` refuses a sink that does not carry the owner marker. |
+| SendGrid Sandbox Mode | **NOT THE VALIDATION PATH.** S1O established from the provider's own documentation that a sandbox request generates no Event Webhook and no Email Activity event, so `I36` could observe nothing. `mail_settings.sandbox_mode.enable` is emitted as a literal `false` and no configuration can change it. |
+| Option B | **NOT BUILT.** One adapter and a `NON_MONETARY_WRITE` send credential keep ADR-024's option A legal. The **third** adapter or the **first** `MONEY_MOVING` credential requires option B, and the registry throws rather than starting. |
+| the production owner ceremony | **NOT PERFORMED.** Step 9 is a NON-PRODUCTION release, and it is still an owner act. |
+
+---
+
+## 1. Create a dedicated non-production sending identity
+
+Create a **separate SendGrid account**, or a **subuser** under an existing one, used for
+nothing else. A subuser is sufficient only if it has its own sending identity and its own
+API keys; a shared key from the parent account is not an isolated identity.
+
+Record its non-secret name — this becomes `environmentLabel` in step 10.
+
+## 2. Confirm it cannot reach production workflows
+
+- no production domain is authenticated on it;
+- no production template, suppression list or contact list is present;
+- no production webhook endpoint is configured against it;
+- the account or subuser sends to nothing but the sink from step 11.
+
+## 3. Provision a verified non-production sender identity
+
+Verify a Single Sender, or authenticate a domain you own that is **not** the production
+sending domain. This address becomes `senderAddress` in step 10.
+
+## 4. Enable and confirm the Email Activity history entitlement
+
+The Email Activity Feed API requires purchased **additional email activity history**
+(Account Details → Your Products → Add-ons). S1O's capability record notes a documented
+30-day tier and a published recommendation to wait ~12 hours after purchase for billing to
+propagate.
+
+**CONFIRM IT EMPIRICALLY BEFORE DECLARING IT**, with step 13's read probe. The harness gate
+`EMAIL_ACTIVITY_ENTITLEMENT_UNCONFIRMED` is satisfied by an environment variable, which means
+it records the operator's declaration; step 13 is what turns that declaration into a
+measurement.
+
+## 5. Create the integration credential — `mail.send` and nothing else
+
+`POST /v3/api_keys` with an explicit `scopes` array of exactly `["mail.send"]`.
+**Omitting the array grants Full Access**, so the array is required, not optional.
+
+Record the **`api_key_id`** the response carries. That id — not the key, not a prefix of it,
+not a hash of it — is the `credentialIdentity` in step 10.
+
+## 6. Create the audit credential — `email_activity.read` and nothing else
+
+A **second** key from the same endpoint with `scopes: ["email_activity.read"]`. It must not
+carry `mail.send`. Record its `api_key_id` too.
+
+## 7. Establish the material-bound identity for each credential
+
+`50 §2g` field 1 requires "the stable non-secret identity of the EXACT MATERIAL the runtime
+may present". Two forms are admissible and the source accepts only those two:
+
+| `identityProvenance` | What you write in `credentialIdentity` |
+|---|---|
+| `PROVIDER_KEY_ID` | SendGrid's own `api_key_id` for that key, read back from `GET /v3/api_keys` |
+| `DEPLOYMENT_SECRET_VERSION` | an **immutable** secret-manager version identity for that exact material — one that changes when the material changes |
+
+**A friendly alias, an environment-variable name, a key prefix, a hash or a fingerprint is
+NOT admissible**, and `SYNTHETIC_TEST_IDENTITY` is refused by the live sources
+(`validation/sendgrid/integration/secretSource.ts`,
+`validation/sendgrid/audit/secretSource.ts`). If the credential source available to you
+cannot establish one of the two forms above, **STOP**: the live provider portion of S1P stays
+PARTIAL rather than proceeding on a label.
+
+## 8. Prepare the class-3 and class-5 release candidate
+
+**THIS IS THE STEP THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET, AND S1P DOES NOT CREATE IT.**
+
+A SendGrid runtime cannot be registered until the **signed** control artifacts name it:
+
+| Artifact | What it must gain | Refusal without it |
+|---|---|---|
+| class 3, action catalogue | an email action class and the `sendgrid_email` adapter it routes to | `ADAPTER_NOT_IN_CATALOGUE` |
+| class 5, credential scopes | a `NON_MONETARY_WRITE` record for the `mail.send` credential bound to that adapter, and a `READ_ONLY`, `audit_plane`-scoped record for the `email_activity.read` credential | `CREDENTIAL_NOT_DECLARED` on both planes |
+
+**Adding an action class is not a bounded S1P change** — the class-3 parser requires the
+catalogue to be exhaustive over `ACTION_CLASSES` in both directions, and a fifth class reaches
+class 2 (Cedar), class 19 (effect constructors), enumeration, exposure and the accepted
+architecture verification. `tests/sendgrid/prerequisites-and-separation.test.ts` asserts both
+refusals against the deployed bytes so the prerequisite is a failure that fires rather than a
+paragraph. See `S1P-owner-clarifications.md` `S1P-C1`.
+
+Build the candidate with the accepted S1L tooling (`npm run release:control`) once the owner
+has decided the catalogue question.
+
+## 9. Complete the non-production owner approval
+
+The accepted ceremony: a deterministic release candidate, a **primary owner** approval and a
+**separate second-factor** approval, a signed release package and a deployment trust output.
+
+**This is a NON-PRODUCTION release and it is still an owner act.** Do not reuse production
+roots, do not forge either signature, and do not perform the production ceremony.
+
+## 10. Load each credential through its own separate source
+
+Two documents, on the deployment host, **outside this repository**, at paths only the
+operator chooses.
+
+Integration document — the path goes in `ACOS_S1P_INTEGRATION_LOCATOR`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "adapterId": "sendgrid_email",
+  "apiKey": "<the mail.send key — NEVER commit this>",
+  "credentialIdentity": "<the api_key_id from step 5>",
+  "identityProvenance": "PROVIDER_KEY_ID",
+  "version": "2026-09-27",
+  "revoked": false,
+  "senderAddress": "<the verified sender from step 3>",
+  "sinkAddress": "<the owner-controlled sink from step 11>",
+  "environmentLabel": "<the name from step 1>"
+}
+```
+
+Audit document — the path goes in `ACOS_S1P_AUDIT_LOCATOR`, and it is a **different file**:
+
+```json
+{
+  "providerId": "twilio_sendgrid",
+  "apiKey": "<the email_activity.read key — NEVER commit this>",
+  "credentialIdentity": "<the api_key_id from step 6>",
+  "identityProvenance": "PROVIDER_KEY_ID",
+  "version": "2026-09-27",
+  "revoked": false
+}
+```
+
+`createAuditReaderRegistry` refuses `READER_LOCATOR_SHARED_WITH_INTEGRATION` if the two
+locators are the same, and the preflight refuses `CREDENTIALS_NOT_DISTINCT` if the two
+identities are.
+
+**Revocation is `"revoked": true` in the document.** It is a state of the deployment
+boundary, re-read on every resolve, and it returns no material at all.
+
+## 11. Configure an owner-controlled sink, and nothing else
+
+The sink address must contain `acos-nonprod-sink` — a sub-address tag such as
+`you+acos-nonprod-sink@yourdomain.test` is the intended form. `isOwnerControlledSink` refuses
+anything else, at configuration time AND again at mapping time.
+
+**No customer address. No shared inbox. No distribution list.**
+
+## 12. Run the credential-identity preflight
+
+```bash
+ACOS_S1P_INTEGRATION_LOCATOR=/path/int.json ACOS_S1P_AUDIT_LOCATOR=/path/aud.json npm run validate:sendgrid
+```
+
+With no acknowledgement set, this refuses on `LIVE_RUN_NOT_OPTED_IN` and makes **no provider
+call**. Read the gate list: every other gate should already be clear before you opt in.
+
+## 13. Run the provider scope/capability probes
+
+The probes measure what each credential can actually do at the provider. **They conclude
+nothing from a key's name, its id, its label or the request that created it.**
+
+| Probe | Credential | Required result |
+|---|---|---|
+| `GET /v3/messages` | audit | `CAPABILITY_CONFIRMED` |
+| `GET /v3/messages` | integration | `CAPABILITY_REFUSED_BY_PROVIDER` (a correctly scoped `mail.send` key cannot read activity) |
+| `POST /v3/mail/send` | **audit** | `CAPABILITY_REFUSED_BY_PROVIDER` — **this is the `36 §13` attempted-write test** |
+
+A `CAPABILITY_CONFIRMED` on the third row means the audit key can send. That is a
+**provisioning failure**, it is reported as one, and `48 §3.6`'s read-only exemption is not
+earned by that credential.
+
+A 4xx other than 401/403 on the first row commonly means the **entitlement** from step 4 is
+absent rather than the scope — the probe records `INCONCLUSIVE` and says so.
+
+## 14. Run the baseline send/read correlation test
+
+One effect, dispatched through the ordinary claim/dispatch path, observed through the
+independent audit reader on its correlation tag. It establishes that a normal
+`sandbox_mode=false` send on this account is queryable at all — which S1O recorded as
+pending — before the kill-point scenarios depend on it.
+
+**Watch the rate limit.** The Email Activity API publishes 6 requests per minute; the
+observation loop's interval floor is derived from that figure.
+
+## 15. Run the six real `I36` kill-point scenarios
+
+Each scenario uses a **fresh** deterministic effect identity, the owner-controlled sink, the
+normal ACOS claim/dispatch path, and the canonical trigger from
+`validation/sendgrid/harness/killPoints.ts`. Recovery follows the accepted semantics only.
+**No redispatch of CLAIMED work, ever.**
+
+Kill point 3 is produced by launching the integration runtime with
+`validation/sendgrid/integration/killPointAdapter.js` as its `adapterModule` and
+`"killPoint": "EXIT_AFTER_SEND"` in the integration document. `adapter.js` has no such branch.
+
+> **THE SCENARIO DRIVER IS NOT IMPLEMENTED.** The preflight, the adapter, the kill-point
+> adapter, the reader, the observation loop, the kill-point map and the evidence bundle all
+> exist; the code that walks the six scenarios against a live account does not, and writing it
+> blind would be writing fake provider results. The harness exits `2` and says so when the
+> preflight passes.
+
+## 16. Run the audit-key send-refusal test
+
+Step 13's third row, recorded into the bundle as `auditKeySendRefusal` so a reviewer does not
+have to find it among the other probes.
+
+## 17. Optionally run the duplicate-send negative control
+
+**A SECOND, SEPARATE opt-in** (`ACOS_S1P_DUPLICATE_CONTROL_ACKNOWLEDGEMENT`) beyond the
+ordinary live acknowledgement. Its purpose is to prove that the provider-side oracle can
+DETECT a duplicate — not to prove ACOS produced one.
+
+Minimum messages, unique test-only correlation, owner sink only, never through a production
+control-plane API, and clearly labelled as deliberately violating the safe path.
+
+## 18. Collect the sanitized evidence
+
+`artifacts/s1p-validation/<run-id>.json`. The renderer **throws** rather than redacting if a
+secret shape reaches it, so a bundle that exists is a bundle that carried none.
+
+Review it for: both credential identities, both signed-record matches, the probe outcomes, the
+redacted sink, one row per kill point with its expected semantics, local state, provider
+observation, **provider-side accepted count**, recovery and `redispatchOccurred`.
+
+**The local digest anchors nothing.** `I17b` stays open.
+
+## 19. Leave production SendGrid disabled
+
+Remove nothing from production because nothing was added to it. Confirm: the production
+adapter registry is still empty, the deployment documents from step 10 are still outside the
+repository, and the non-production keys are revoked or rotated when the run is finished.

