+# S1P — owner clarifications
+
+Questions this slice could not answer from the mandate plus v1.3.7 alone. Each records the
+gap, what was done, and what a ruling would change.
+
+**`S1P-C1` IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.** It blocks the live run on its own, independently of
+credentials, and it is the reason the live portion would remain PARTIAL even if an account
+existed today.
+
+---
+
+## 0. The clarifications, in one table
+
+| ID | Question | What S1P did | Blocking? |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **C1** | S1P requires a real SendGrid send through the accepted claim/dispatch path. The signed class-3 catalogue names **four action classes and three adapters, none of them email**. Adding an email action class is not a bounded change. | Built everything that does not depend on it; left both registry refusals firing and asserted them | **YES**, for the live run |
+| **C2** | Where does a REAL vendor client live, given the accepted boundaries forbid one under `src/` and `§18` reserves `TEST_ONLY` for synthetic providers? | A fourth perimeter root, `validation/`, scanned at **PRODUCTION** scope; three accepted assertions amended out loud | No |
+| **C3** | May a 4xx from `/v3/mail/send` be classified `NOT_SENT_CONFIRMED / PROVIDER_REJECTED_NO_MUTATION`? | **No.** Every provider answer other than 202 is `ADAPTER_FAILED / PROVIDER_CLIENT_REJECTED` | No — conservative |
+| **C4** | Where does the owner-controlled sink come from, given no email action class exists to carry a recipient? | **Launch configuration**, never message content | No |
+| **C5** | The `36 §13` attempted-write probe needs a send with the audit credential. Where can it live without becoming an audit capability? | `validation/sendgrid/harness/`, outside the audit package, with its own perimeter exemption reason | No |
+
+---
+
+## S1P-C1 — there is no email action class, and adding one is not a bounded S1P change
+
+**The gap.** `§9` of the mandate requires the six kill points to run "through the normal ACOS
+claim/dispatch path" against the real SendGrid boundary. That path begins at an authorised
+effect whose action class the **verified class-3 catalogue** routes to an adapter.
+
+The deployed catalogue carries exactly four action classes —
+
+```
+campaign.pause   refund.create   fulfilment.reship   campaign.budget.set
+```
+
+— routed to exactly three adapters: `mock_ads`, `mock_commerce`, `mock_processor`.
+**None of them is email, and none of them is SendGrid.**
+
+`createAdapterRuntimeRegistry` refuses `ADAPTER_NOT_IN_CATALOGUE` for `sendgrid_email`, and
+`createAuditReaderRegistry` refuses `CREDENTIAL_NOT_DECLARED` for either credential. Both
+refusals are asserted against the deployed bytes in
+`tests/sendgrid/prerequisites-and-separation.test.ts`.
+
+**Why S1P did not simply add one.** `artifactParsers.ts` requires the class-3 artifact to be
+exhaustive over `ACTION_CLASSES` **in both directions** — "the artifact declaring a class
+outside `ACTION_CLASSES`, or omitting one inside it, is a refusal". So a fifth TypeScript
+member forces a fifth catalogue record, and a fifth catalogue record reaches:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| class 3 | recoverability, value direction, monetary field, cost/rate flags, irrecoverable units, settlement tolerance, adapter, method, **plus** `semantic_option_digest_fields` and `enumeration_max_age_seconds`, both keyed per class |
+| class 2 | the Cedar schema and policy set, whose closure `26 §2.3` makes structural |
+| class 19 | an effect constructor, and class-19 key migration is a **currently open obligation** |
+| the manifest | class 3's content hash, the manifest id, the deployment pin and the class-19 admission proof |
+| verification | the accepted 108 PASS / 77 mutation-seed architecture verification |
+
+That is not a bounded slice. It is also, specifically, **an authority decision**: the
+recoverability of "send an email" (it cannot be unsent), its value direction, and whether the
+action belongs in the S1 catalogue at all are owner rulings, not implementation details, and
+`§21` forbids manufacturing one.
+
+**What S1P did instead.** Built every part that does not depend on the catalogue — the
+adapter, the kill-point adapter, the vendor clients, the audit reader, the observation loop,
+the preflight, the probes, the evidence bundle — and left the two refusals firing, asserted,
+and named by the preflight gate `ADAPTER_NOT_IN_SIGNED_CATALOGUE`. `S1P-operator-procedure.md`
+step 8 records it as the operator/owner prerequisite it is.
+
+**What a ruling would change.** A decision to add an email action class turns step 8 into a
+release-candidate exercise with the accepted S1L tooling, plus the class-2/class-19 work the
+table above names. A decision to run `I36` against a non-email action class instead would
+require an adapter whose catalogue entry is honest about what it dispatches, and S1P does not
+believe one exists.
+
+---
+
+## S1P-C2 — where a real vendor client lives
+
+**The gap.** Three accepted assertions say `src/` holds no adapter implementation, no vendor
+name and no real transport, and S1M declined to widen them because it "adds nothing which
+needs the width". S1P adds something that does. But `§18` reserves the `TEST_ONLY` perimeter
+scope for a "test synthetic provider", and a client reaching `api.sendgrid.com` is not one.
+
+**What S1P did.** A fourth perimeter root, `validation/`, whose first path segment is not
+`tests` and whose sites are therefore **PRODUCTION** perimeter entries. `src/` is
+byte-unchanged in every property those three suites assert: no vendor name, no adapter
+implementation, no import of `validation/`, and `emptyAdapterRuntimeRegistry()` still empty.
+
+Three accepted assertions in `perimeter-enumeration.test.ts` were amended, out loud and in
+the file, per `45 §3`:
+
+1. the root list, from three to four;
+2. "every `PROVIDER_CLIENT` site is `TEST_ONLY`" → every site **under `tests/`** is
+   `TEST_ONLY`, **and** the SendGrid send sites are `PRODUCTION` and `AUTHORISED`;
+3. "every `PROVIDER_READ_CLIENT` site is `TEST_ONLY`" → the annotation requirement is
+   unchanged and asserted by value in both scopes; the scope requirement applies to the
+   synthetic reader, and the real one is a `PRODUCTION` entry.
+
+Plus one extension: "no send client lives in the audit plane" now covers
+`validation/sendgrid/audit/` as well, and the scope probes must live in `harness/`.
+
+**What a ruling would change.** A decision that the SendGrid adapter belongs under `src/`
+would require narrowly widening `no-real-transport-boundary.test.ts` and the
+`postmark-tooling-boundary.test.ts` `src/` scans, and would put a live vendor client in the
+production control-plane tree while production's registry must stay empty. S1P judged that
+the worse trade and recorded the reasoning in `validation/sendgrid/README.md`.
+
+---
+
+## S1P-C3 — `NOT_SENT_CONFIRMED` is not reachable from any SendGrid status
+
+**The gap.** `25 §7.2` admits `NOT_SENT_CONFIRMED / PROVIDER_REJECTED_NO_MUTATION` for "a
+provider rejection whose declared adapter contract **guarantees** no external mutation
+occurred". A 400 or a 403 from `/v3/mail/send` looks like exactly that.
+
+**What S1P did.** Refused it. `36 §7` requires the guarantee to be ESTABLISHED against the
+vendor, Twilio SendGrid publishes no such statement, and S1P measured nothing. Every provider
+answer other than 202 is `ADAPTER_FAILED / PROVIDER_CLIENT_REJECTED`, which carries no local
+outcome policy and therefore claims nothing; 5xx and 429 are `OUTCOME_UNKNOWN`.
+
+The cost is stated rather than engineered away, as `35 §12.3` states the analogous one: a
+request SendGrid definitely refused is treated as one that might not have been.
+
+**What a ruling would change.** Nothing in S1P. A measured vendor contract from a later slice
